WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:11.261
When I was a kid walking down the beach, 

00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:16.891
I could see so many fish along the seashore, the beach... 

00:00:37.662 --> 00:00:41.207
My name is Juan Castro Montaño.

00:00:41.207 --> 00:00:48.298
I am 71 years old and I've always lived here, in Cabo Pulmo.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:57.015
Fishing was very important for this community because 
that was our means of living. 

00:01:00.518 --> 00:01:05.023
I learned how to fish at age 12.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:12.447
Then at 15, I became a professional fisherman.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:16.910
Slowly, commercial fishing began. 

00:01:20.121 --> 00:01:27.378
We noticed that there was massive over-fishing.

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.847
That was the worst thing we could have done. 

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:46.689
It was visibly noticeable that the fish population was declining 

00:01:46.689 --> 00:01:52.403
and that fishing here was no longer profitable.

00:01:52.403 --> 00:01:58.118
There weren't enough fish here to survive.

00:02:23.393 --> 00:02:29.357
We stopped fishing from one day to the next. 

00:02:29.357 --> 00:02:37.782
Since we saw how difficult it was to fish there, our generation, 
my brothers and I were the ones who immediately said yes. 

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:45.039
We needed to change this, because fishing was very difficult. 
We invested a lot and we practically didn't get anything back. 

00:02:47.292 --> 00:02:51.713
Now we work in eco-tourism.

00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:58.595
It was very, very difficult but we made it.

00:02:58.595 --> 00:03:07.145
It is a way of preserving the reef and dedicating ourselves 
to something else, 

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:19.574
They began to prefer transporting tourists because they knew 
they got paid well and they consumed less fuel. 

00:03:19.574 --> 00:03:25.580
Besides, they were home early and that's what encouraged them 
to give up the fishing. 

00:03:26.998 --> 00:03:30.919
We all benefit from this reef. 

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:35.381
It has already recovered, not 100 percent...

00:03:38.551 --> 00:03:40.428
... 500 percent.

00:04:01.366 --> 00:04:11.417
I think that my dad, my grandfather, seeing how the reef has recovered, 
would think of how it was when they were young.

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:15.838
And they would say: 
“It came back. The riches that we had as kids came back.”

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:28.351
I think they would have been very proud.

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:36.818
Other generations will see this when we're gone.

00:04:36.818 --> 00:04:42.949
We're the sentinels, watching over, and taking care of it.

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:53.918
And I tell the children: "I wish part of what I am telling you now remains 
deeply etched into your minds and hearts, 

00:04:56.129 --> 00:05:05.847
so that even if there are no books talking about this, at least  
you will keep the story alive once I am gone."

